<topic xml:id="rt-hardware-testing-next-steps"
       role="glue" xml:lang="en"
       xmlns="http://docbook.org/ns/docbook" version="5.2"
       xmlns:its="http://www.w3.org/2005/11/its"
       xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"
       xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
       xmlns:trans="http://docbook.org/ns/transclusion">
  <info>
    <title>Next steps</title>
    <meta name="daria.vladykina@suse.com" content="" its:translate="no"/>
    <abstract>
      <para>
        Completing latency benchmarks is only the first part of system preparation. This section
        details essential post-testing tasks to optimize your real-time server. It guides you to
        document your system configuration and perform regular regression testing.
      </para>
    </abstract>
  </info>
  <para>
    Latency testing is only one step in preparing your system. After completing your measurements,
    analyze the results and tune your system.
  </para>
  <para>
    Document your test environment thoroughly. Record the following:
  </para>
  <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        server model
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        processor details
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        firmware versions
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        enabled features
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        operating system version
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        running kernel
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        tool options
      </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
      <para>
        workloads
      </para>
    </listitem>
  </itemizedlist>
  <para>
    Repeat your latency testing after making significant system changes. Regular retesting helps
    identify performance regressions before they affect production workloads.
  </para>
</topic>
